# Rule: every PTY runs in the pty host, never in main

Every terminal byte used to cross Electron's main process: node-pty's synchronous spawn, the
headless xterm parse of every chunk, the raw transcript strip and insert, and each adapter's output
detectors. Measured under a 15 s terminal flood, main was 40.6% busy, ran 30,507 `pty:data` spans,
and its event-loop delay reached 43 to 46 ms. VS Code runs node-pty in a utility process for the
same reason. So every PTY moved to the `kangentic-pty-host` utility process
(`src/main/pty/host/pty-host-entry.ts`), and the same flood now costs main 0.6 to 0.7% with its delay
at the Windows timer floor.

## The rule

- **The host core owns every session PTY and the per-chunk work.** `PtyHostCore`
  (`src/main/pty/host/pty-host-core.ts`) holds node-pty, the headless buffer and scrollback ring,
  backpressure, the raw transcript writer, and the adapters' output detectors. Main reaches it only
  through `PtyHostClient` (commands, id-matched requests, ordered events; `protocol.ts`).
  `SessionManager` stays the facade, so `sessionManager.spawn(` and every other rule's call shape
  are unchanged.
- **Main keeps mirrors, not the data.** What main reads synchronously (alt-screen state, buffer
  width) is mirrored from host events. Everything else is an async request: scrollback, the serialized
  frame, the phone's seed frame (`getSeedFrame`), raw scrollback, the output peek, diagnostics. Do not add a synchronous read of host state
  to main.
- **Terminal input and echo never cross main.** A keystroke used to reach the host through main's
  IPC and its echo came back the same way, so a git start or a `node_modules` deletion on main
  held every terminal: echo p99 293 ms and max 454 ms under a worktree create. Each renderer now
  holds its own port to the host, one end of a `MessageChannelMain` that `ipc/handlers/sessions.ts`
  brokers when the preload asks (`PTY_PORT_REQUEST`) and never relays. The preload keeps its end
  (`src/preload/pty-port-client.ts`) behind the unchanged `ElectronAPI` shape. Keystrokes and acks
  go to the host on it, and the host sends a session's output only to the ports of the renderers
  that show it (`setRendererFocus`, kept per renderer). A scrollback request still goes through
  main, behind that renderer's resizes, and the host answers on the asking port after the output
  it flushed before the sample. The host owns the write queue and the prompt-draft ledger, and
  main mirrors the draft from `promptDraft`. Every place main drops a session's PTY handle calls
  `sealUserInput()` first, so a renderer's keystrokes stop reaching an agent in its exit. A host
  restart issues every renderer a new port before it replays each renderer's focus set. The
  in-process fallback keeps main's end, so typing crosses main only there.
- **A phone and everything else stay off the ports.** The host sends `tap` for sessions a phone
  streams (`subscribeDataTap`, ref-counted) and a coalesced `outputSeen` (no bytes) for everything
  else. A new consumer of raw output subscribes a tap for as long as it needs one; it never gets a
  renderer's port.
- **Chromium's deferral after input stays on.** Chromium holds a posted message until the frame
  after a keystroke has painted (`DeferRendererTasksAfterInput`), so an idle echo paints one frame
  later than one that crossed main. Do not turn the feature off to win that frame back. Measured
  off, React's own scheduled work ran ahead of a drag's drop frame too: the drop painted 15 to
  62 ms after release instead of 29 to 40, with seven long frames in six drags.
- **The host's I/O thread starts no process.** On Windows CreateProcess runs synchronously on the
  calling thread, and under disk load a git start took 190 to 454 ms there, freezing every
  terminal. Every one-shot child (`runHostExec`), agent CLI run (`host-cli-processes.ts`) and
  process-table tool (`ps`, `lsappinfo`, the PowerShell fallback, `taskkill`) starts on the host's
  spawn worker instead, through `HostSpawnService`. The worker (`host/spawn-worker/`, built as
  `pty-host-spawn-worker.js` and unpacked) is a `worker_threads` Worker that loads only `node:`
  built-ins and pure modules; main hands the host its path in `init`. The I/O thread still pins a
  run's pid for a tree stop (`pinRun`). After three worker crashes, or a worker that fails to
  start, the host starts processes itself again. node-pty is the one exception: its handle belongs
  to the thread that spawned it, so a PTY spawn stays on the I/O thread (`pty:spawn`, 5 to 38 ms
  measured under load).
- **One host, forked once, built in two places only.** `register-all.ts` constructs the
  `UtilityPtyHostTransport`; `SessionManager.createInProcessHost` is the only place the core runs in
  main, as the unit tests' host and as the fallback after five host crashes.
- **A shell that exits on its own has its leftovers ended in the host (Windows).** The host core
  asks the agent the dead shell left running to exit through that shell's still-open console, then
  terminates what is left after the grace, by parentage and creation time (`dead-root-orphans.ts`,
  see [[pty-teardown-grace]]). The shell's own lifetime comes off the handle the host holds on
  every Windows PTY root from its pid to its exit (`pinRoot`), which also keeps node-pty's kill off
  a reused pid. Main sends each session's exit sequence in the spawn params for it.
  Only the utility host is given the Windows API (`loadOrphanApi` in `pty-host-entry.ts`), so the
  in-process core, the tests' and the post-crash fallback's, never runs it or pins anything.
- **A host crash ends its PTYs and recovery resumes them.** Main first stops each lost PTY's
  process tree by pid (`stopLostPtyTree`, which only the utility transport implements): a closed
  pseudo console or a hangup usually ends the agent, but nothing guarantees it, and one left running
  would edit its worktree beside the resumed session. Each lost PTY is then reported through its
  own exit listener with `PTY_HOST_LOST_EXIT_CODE` (-2), the host restarts (at once, then on the
  restart policy's backoff), main replays its focus and tap sets, and
  `recoverSessionsAfterPtyHostLoss` resumes the agent sessions through the startup recovery path,
  then starts fresh the lost tasks that had no agent session id to resume, as startup does.
- **macOS forks the host from inside the asar** (`ptyHostEntryPath`). node-pty finds its
  spawn-helper with a bare `replace('app.asar', 'app.asar.unpacked')` on its own path, which from
  the unpacked tree doubles and breaks every spawn. Windows and Linux fork from the unpacked tree,
  where the ConPTY conout worker loads from a real directory. The afterPack probe loads node-pty
  the same way per platform, and `spawn-helper-upstream-parity.test.ts` pins both.
- **The host stays free of main-only modules.** No `electron` import, no IPC layer, no analytics,
  no Sentry, no retrieval code. It writes transcripts on its own database connection (migrations
  off, `wal_autocheckpoint` 0: the retrieval worker runs the checkpoints).
- **Quit.** `killAll()` posts every kill (a young session's after its grace, see
  [[pty-teardown-grace]]) and adds the host's pid to the drain report; `dispose()` posts `shutdown`;
  the host flushes, waits for every exit callback, and exits itself. See [[synchronous-shutdown]].
- **Project delete awaits `closeProjectInPtyHost`** before unlinking the database files, as it does
  the retrieval worker's close.
- **One-shot child processes run in the host too.** On Windows libuv runs CreateProcess
  synchronously on the calling thread, 15 to 30 ms a spawn; agent detection alone ran 30 of them on
  main at startup, 17 at 16 ms or more. Use `execAsync` / `execFileAsync` from
  `src/main/utility-process/off-main-exec.ts`, never `promisify(exec)` or `promisify(execFile)`:
  they resolve and reject exactly as `promisify` does, run the child in the host (`host-exec.ts`,
  on its spawn worker), and fall back to a local spawn where no host is registered or reachable. The host never forks and
  refuses to launch its own executable or main's (the init message carries `mainExecutable`, since
  on macOS the host runs from the Helper bundle), either of which, with the RunAsNode fuse off,
  would boot a second app. A session's or a probe's PTY whose program is one of them is refused
  the same way.
  The background-shell watcher's process table also comes from the host (`listProcesses`,
  `host-process-table.ts`): a Toolhelp snapshot through koffi on Windows (about 8 ms, against
  140 ms for the PowerShell CIM query it replaced, which the host still starts, for good, the
  first time the Toolhelp listing fails), `ps` on POSIX. So does the task leftover reap (`reapTaggedProcesses`,
  `src/main/pty/process-tag/`): the host scans for the `KANGENTIC_TASK_ID` tag and kills, and on
  Windows and macOS it loads koffi (an esbuild external, unpacked, and loaded by the afterPack
  probe) to read another process's environment (the PEB; the `KERN_PROCARGS2` record), the
  machine's TCP table on Windows (`GetExtendedTcpTable`), and, on macOS, the process list, working
  directories and each process's sockets from libproc. The scan yields to the event loop
  as it goes, and a native call long enough to hold it (the Toolhelp snapshot) runs on the thread
  pool through koffi's async call. See
  [[task-process-tag]].
- **Agent CLI runs start in the host too.** `spawnCli` (`src/main/agent/shared/cli-print.ts`)
  starts every headless run (Ask, task summaries, auto-name, the warm answer session) through
  `spawnOffMainCli` (`off-main-cli.ts`): a `cliSpawn` command, so the handle (`RemoteCliProcess`)
  comes back at once and emits what a local child does, `spawn`, `data`, `exit`, `close`, in the
  host's order. A summary backfill started the CLI 39 times in 100 s at 13 to 67 ms each on main;
  in the host the first, cold launch measured 51 ms and later ones stayed under 16 ms. Once posted
  a run never falls back to a local spawn, and a host crash fails it with `error`, `exit`, `close`
  rather than rerunning it (a second paid answer); main then stops the orphaned CLI's tree, since
  on Windows a child outlives its parent. Stops run in the host (`taskkill /T /F` on the spawn
  worker, with the run's pid pinned by the I/O thread's handle until taskkill exits, or the process
  group's SIGTERM then SIGKILL), and the host's shutdown stops every run still going and waits for
  them inside its exit bound. At quit, `stopAllCliRuns` also stops each run's tree from main by pid
  (`killChildTreeByPid`): with no terminal open the quit does not wait for the host, which can be
  torn down before it reads its stop. `spawnCli` spawns locally only when no host is registered.
  Every one of these stops, local, in the host, and by pid, is `src/main/shared/child-tree-stop.ts`,
  which imports only `spawn` so the host can bundle it.

## Enforcement (self-maintaining)

- **Test:** `tests/unit/pty-host-boundary.test.ts` builds the host entry with an esbuild metafile
  (dev and production) and fails on `electron`, the IPC layer, analytics, Sentry, retrieval or dev
  tooling in its graph; pins the build entry in both `scripts/build.js` and `scripts/dev.js`; pins
  the one construction site of each transport and of `PtyHostCore`; fails on a value import of
  `node-pty` outside the host core, and on a lazy `import('node-pty')` outside the
  `off-main-pty.ts` fallback; fails on `promisify(exec)` / `promisify(execFile)` under
  `src/main` outside the drop-in and its one reasoned exception (`shell-env.ts`); pins that `host-exec.ts` and
  `host-cli-processes.ts` never fork and check for their own executable; pins that `spawnCli`
  asks `spawnOffMainCli` before its local fallback, which is the only spawn in `cli-print.ts`; and
  pins that `child-tree-stop.ts` never forks, spawns nothing but `taskkill`, imports only `spawn`,
  and is the only place a CLI tree stop builds a `taskkill`. Runs in CI via `npm run test:unit`.
  `tests/unit/child-tree-stop.test.ts` pins each stop branch per platform.
- **Test (input and echo):** `tests/unit/terminal-input-path.test.ts` fails when an IPC channel
  carries a keystroke, the echo or its ack again, when main emits or listens for a renderer `data`
  event, when the preload's `write`, `onData`, `ackData` or `getScrollback` touches
  `ipcRenderer`, when main stops brokering the port or reissuing it after a restart, when
  `useTerminal` asks for the scrollback outside its grid request's continuation, when a kill
  path drops a PTY handle without sealing it, and when anything under `src/main` names
  `DeferRendererTasksAfterInput`. `tests/unit/pty-port-client.test.ts` pins the
  preload's client (writes kept until the port, the answer before the output after it, unknown ids
  dropped, a port swap failing what the dead host never answered).
  `tests/unit/pty-host-core.test.ts` pins the host side: output only to the ports that show the
  session, one write queue for a renderer's keystrokes and main's writes, the seal, the scrollback
  answer's place among the output, and the draft mirror.
  `tests/e2e/terminal-input-second-renderer.spec.ts` types into a terminal in the detached Agent
  Monitor and reads the echo there, then closes it and types into the main window's, so two real
  webContents each get their own port and closing one forgets only that one.
- **Test (spawn worker):** `tests/unit/pty-host-spawn-worker.test.ts` builds the real worker, runs
  an exec and a CLI run through `PtyHostCore`, and fails when the test thread starts a process;
  `pty-host-boundary.test.ts` builds the worker's graph and fails on a native module or a main-only
  module in it, and pins its build entries and `asarUnpack`; `event-loop-watchdog.test.ts` names
  it among the `worker_threads` importers; `verify-unpacked-worker.test.ts` runs the afterPack
  gate, which starts the worker under this checkout's Electron and runs one child through it.
  `tests/unit/off-main-exec.test.ts` pins the drop-in's routing, error shape and fallback;
  `tests/unit/host-cli-processes.test.ts` runs real children through the host's runner (pipes,
  event order, stop, shutdown, a failed start, the own-executable refusal);
  `tests/unit/remote-cli-process.test.ts` pins main's handle (event order, stdin, stop, host loss
  and the orphan stop); `tests/unit/spawn-cli-off-main.test.ts` pins `spawnCli`'s routing and runs
  a headless print end to end through the host's runner.
- **Tests:** `tests/unit/pty-host-core.test.ts` pins the core's behavior;
  `tests/unit/utility-pty-host-transport.test.ts` pins the fork, init, request timeout (its
  rejection types, the budget a queued reap or Stop gets back on `batch-started`, and the late
  answer of a request that timed out), crash
  restart, fallback, heartbeat and shutdown; `tests/unit/verify-unpacked-worker.test.ts` pins the
  packaging (all of `node_modules/node-pty/**` and `.vite/build/pty-host.js` unpacked) and runs the
  afterPack probe's real spawn under this checkout's Electron.
- **Packaging gate:** `build/afterPack.js` resolves node-pty from the unpacked tree and spawns a
  real process with it under the packaged Electron binary, on Windows and macOS loads koffi
  from the same place and makes one native call, and starts the spawn worker and runs one child
  through it, failing the build when any of them cannot.
- **Packaged smoke:** `.github/workflows/package-smoke.yml` packages on Windows, macOS and Linux
  when a pull request touches the host, its clients or the packaging, and runs
  `scripts/package-smoke.mjs`: a terminal in the finished app, a Knowledge Graph read from the
  retrieval worker, a task reap that must stop a detached process a task's terminal left (koffi
  loading in the packaged host), a quit with the terminal running (a user's quit on Windows and Linux; SIGTERM
  on macOS, which skips the exit drain), and a fail on any log line saying a forked process
  crashed or the host fell back to main. Not a required check.
  `tests/unit/package-smoke.test.ts` pins the script's pure parts and that every log line it
  watches for still exists in `src/main`.

## Scope

Session PTYs and their output pipeline (`src/main/pty/**`), the host's fork and wiring in
`register-all.ts`, each renderer's port (`src/preload/pty-port-client.ts` and the broker in
`ipc/handlers/sessions.ts`), one-shot child processes on main, the probes' raw PTYs (the Claude model
picker, the Antigravity print runner spawn through `spawnOffMainPty`, `off-main-pty.ts`), the
agent CLI runs (`spawnCli`, `off-main-cli.ts`), and the packaging.

Git on a task's lifecycle runs in the host too (#732): a workflow runs up to eight lifecycles at
once, and their git spawns stalled main 19 times over 75 ms in a 20-task run. A start, a respawning
move and a move to Done go through `src/main/git/git-client.ts` (`gitAt`, `runGitCommand`, which
keep simple-git's method names and results; simple-git has no spawn hook) and
`spawn-with-abort.ts`, which routes a git command to `spawnOffMainCli`. Measured by
`tests/unit/git-lifecycle-off-main.test.ts`: 37 git processes per modeled lifecycle, none on main,
against 49 with 45 on main before. Three calls off the lifecycle use the git client too, because
they run repository hooks, filters or merge drivers and `gitAt`'s env drops Crashpad's pipe name
(`src/main/shared/child-env.ts`): Update from base's merge, the carry of uncommitted changes into a
new worktree, and the Command Terminal's cold-start checkout and merge. Still on main, because none
runs in a lifecycle: the branch summary's ahead and behind count, the diff watcher, blame, file
history, the commit graph, the worktree list, a task's branch switch config write, and the Done
drop's pending-changes probe. A new git call on the lifecycle path uses the git client;
the lifecycle test fails when one lands on main.
